<h2>What Attributes Do You See?</h2><p>Grade3 shapes are classified by observable attributes. An angle is informally a corner; a right angle is square-like. Teacher uses actual shape cards, not invented publisher diagrams.</p>
<h3>Warm-up: Which One Doesn't Belong?</h3><p>Look at original four figures A–D, choose one shape that doesn't belong and justify by accurate visible sides, corner type, lengths or shading. Other choices are also possible if explained.</p>
<h3>Card Sort — two original steps</h3><ol><li>In PAIRS sort the original Shape Cards into categories of your own, explain each criterion and note cards may fit different groups.</li><li>Then take turns SECRETLY sorting cards into exactly TWO NEW categories without revealing rule; partner guesses the rule. Swap roles.</li></ol>
<p>Teacher's source text verifies card O has FOUR angles and cards B,F,M,S contain right angles. Source synthesis compares A (three sides, no right angles) and S (five sides, two right angles) to discuss valid different grouping criteria. The original card picture pixels remain unverified.</p>
<p>Teacher checks mathematical precision of self-chosen categories, ability to state rules and use informal Grade3 angle/right-angle vocabulary. Original blackline master and gated Cool-down not reproduced.</p></section>